  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery reveals a mix of sun and clouds \nacross eastern Idaho, while regional radar tracks light returns \nacross parts of the Central Mountains up toward the Montana \nDivide. Areas farther south across the Snake Plain and Magic \nValley remain mostly clear. Temperatures continue to run on the \nwarm side of seasonal climatology, with lower elevations peaking\nin the upper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nA pattern change is already underway to our southwest as an \nupper-level low moves onshore along the central California \ncoast. Between now and the main event, most locations will stay \ndry. However, persistent southerly flow will allow high- \nresolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) to spark isolated \nshowers and a few embedded thunderstorms through the early \novernight hours tonight. A noticeable lull in precipitation will\nthen take hold for much of the daytime hours on Thursday, \nproviding a dry window ahead of the primary system.\n\nThe California upper low will track across the Great Basin over\nthe next 24 to 48 hours, spreading deep moisture and strong \nsynoptic lift directly into eastern Idaho. Widespread rainfall \nwill begin moving into southern and western zones late Thursday \nevening, expanding areawide through the day on Friday.\n\nWhile model guidance continues to exhibit minor spread on \nlocalized placement\u2014nudging rainfall totals upward in upslope- \nfavored areas around Sun Valley and slightly downward across the\nSnake Plain\u2014the big picture remains unchanged: widespread, \nsoaking precipitation is imminent. Due to the total forecast \nprecipitation, a Flash Flood Watch has been issued for the \nWapiti, Ohio, and Big Rock burn scars given the elevated threat \nfor flash flooding and debris flows over hydrophobic soils. More\nwidespread areal flooding remains a low-end possibility if \nprecipitation overperforms current consensus forecasts, but \nbroad areal flood statements are not warranted at this time.\n\nStrong cold advection and extensive cloud cover will result in \na significantly colder Friday. Daytime maximum temperatures will\nstruggle to climb into the 50s across lower elevations, though \na few valley spots may briefly reach that threshold during brief\nbreaks. Rain will steadily taper off from west to east late \nFriday evening and into the early morning hours on Saturday as \nthe low center pulls eastward.\n\nA much more pleasant weekend is in store once morning clouds\nexit on Saturday. While Saturday will remain on the cooler side,\nsunshine will break out during the second half of the day and\ncontinue into Sunday. Daytime high temperatures will hold at or\nslightly below seasonal averages in the 50s and low 60s. No\nwidespread organized storm systems are projected behind this\ndeparting low, though isolated showers will remain possible\nacross higher-terrain zones Monday and Tuesday under a cool,\nseasonable regime.",
